Why GPL-3.0-or-later
--------------------

Onym Core statically links the bundled xdrpp library (lib/xdrpp), which its
author, David Mazieres, licenses to the general public under GPL-3.0-or-later
(see lib/xdrpp/COPYING). That license lets the Stellar Development Foundation
relicense xdrpp, but grants no such right to Onym Core. Onym Core therefore
distributes the combined work under GPL-3.0-or-later.

How the parts are licensed
--------------------------

1. Upstream Stellar Core code. Files inherited from Stellar Core
   (https://github.com/stellar/stellar-core) remain Copyright Stellar
   Development Foundation and contributors, under the Apache License, Version
   2.0. See COPYING and LICENSE-APACHE.txt. Their copyright and license headers
   are kept unchanged. The Apache License 2.0 is compatible with GPLv3, so
   these files may be distributed as part of the GPL-3.0-or-later whole. Their
   Apache-2.0 terms, including its attribution and notice requirements, still
   apply to them.

2. Onym Core contributions. Original work by the Onym Core contributors,
   including Onym's changes to upstream files, is licensed under
   GPL-3.0-or-later.

3. Third-party components. Bundled libraries and git submodules under lib/ and
   src/rust/soroban/, and the Rust crates in Cargo.lock, keep their own
   licenses (MIT, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, Boost-1.0, ISC, Apache-2.0,
   Unicode, public domain, and GPL-3.0-or-later for xdrpp). All of them are
   compatible with GPLv3. See COPYING, NOTICE and each component's own license
   file.

Distributing binaries
---------------------

Anyone who distributes onym-core binaries must comply with GPLv3, including
section 6: provide the Complete Corresponding Source for that binary. That
source is this repository at the exact commit built, including every git
submodule at its recorded revision and the build scripts (autogen.sh,
configure.ac, Makefile.am files, Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock). Releases should
also keep the license notices listed above.
